# Rust Code Generation Status

This file is regenerated on each build.  Do not put your implementations here;
see "Where to Put Your Code" below.

## Java Code in the Grammar

The generator translates the Java code embedded in the grammar to Rust: code
actions, semantic lookaheads (`SCAN {...} =>`), assertions (`ENSURE`/`ASSERT`),
tree-building conditions (`#Node(condition)`), production parameters and
arguments, lexical actions, and the fields and methods injected with
`INJECT PARSER_CLASS` and `INJECT LEXER_CLASS` (which become members of the
`Parser` and `Lexer` structs).  It translates only what it can translate
faithfully.  Everything else is reported when the parser is generated: as an
error when it affects parsing (the generated code then contains a
`compile_error!` with the message), or as a warning.  With the generator option
`-rust-allow-stubs`, those errors become warnings and the code is replaced by a
stub, so the Rust parser may behave differently from the Java parser.

### Translation report

${globals.rustTranslationReport()}

### The Java subset that is translated

* **Types:** `boolean`, `int`, `long`, `char`, `String` literals, the grammar's
  `TokenType` and `LexicalState`, tokens, and the parser's call stack entries
  and iterators (`NonTerminalCall`, `ListIterator<NonTerminalCall>`).  Fields
  can be of type `boolean`, `int`, `long`, `char`, `TokenType` or `Token`,
  with a constant initializer; `static final` fields are allowed (every parser
  or lexer has its own copy).
  Floating point, arrays, generic collections and other classes are not
  supported; nor are `String` fields or string concatenation.
* **Statements:** local variable declarations, assignments (including chained
  and compound assignments), `++`/`--`, `if`/`else`, `while`, `do`/`while`
  (without `continue`), classic `switch` statements without fall-through,
  unlabelled `break` and `continue`, `return` (in injected methods), and
  `assert` (translated to `debug_assert!`).
* **Expressions:** Java's numeric promotion, casts to `int` and `long` (not to
  `char`: a Java `char` is a UTF-16 code unit, a Rust `char` a Unicode scalar
  value), the ternary operator, comparisons, `instanceof` on token classes (no
  patterns), comparisons of `matchedToken` and of token types with `null`,
  `ordinal()` and `equals(type)` on token types, and calls of the injected
  methods.  Integer arithmetic uses Rust's operators, so
  an overflow of `+`, `-`, `*` or unary `-` panics in a debug build (and wraps
  around in a release build), and `MIN_VALUE / -1` and `MIN_VALUE % -1` always
  panic, where Java always wraps around; shifts mask their distance as in
  Java.
* **The parser API:** `getToken(n)`, `getTokenType(n)`,
  `checkNextTokenType(type)`, `isInProduction(names...)`,
  `stackIteratorForward()`, `stackIteratorBackward()`, the iterators'
  `hasNext()`/`next()`/`hasPrevious()`/`previous()`, and the `productionName`
  of a call stack entry.
* **Tokens:** `getType()`, `getBeginOffset()`, `getEndOffset()`, `length()`,
  `isUnparsed()` and `charAt(i)`; in lexical actions, also `truncate(n)`,
  `setBeginOffset(n)`, `setEndOffset(n)` and `replaceType(type)`.
* **The lexer API in lexical actions:** `matchedToken` (which can be set to
  `null` to drop the token), `matchedType`, `matchStart`, `matchEnd`,
  `tokenBeginOffset`, `charAt(i)`, `switchTo(state)`, and the
  `Character.isXxx` predicates.

Code that is not in this subset can be given in Rust instead, in a raw code
block (`{R% ... %}`) placed inside `${"#"}if __rust__ ... ${"#"}endif` when the
grammar also targets other languages.  (A semantic lookahead can instead have
one raw code block per language, such as `SCAN {J% count > 1 %} {R% self.count > 1 %} =>`:
each target uses the block written for it.)  The Rust code is copied as it is
into a method of the generated parser or lexer, where:

* **In the parser** (code actions, semantic lookahead, assertions), `self` is
  the `Parser`.  The fields injected with `INJECT PARSER_CLASS` are
  `self.<name>`, with the name in snake_case (`tokenCount` becomes
  `self.token_count`); production parameters and the locals declared in the
  production's code blocks are Rust variables named the same way.  The parser
  API is `self.get_token(n)` (a `Token`), `self.get_token_type(n)` (a
  `TokenType`, such as `TokenType::ID`), `self.check_next_token_type(t)` and
  `self.is_in_production(&["Name"])`.  A semantic lookahead or an assertion is
  a `bool` expression; a code action is a sequence of statements.  A code action
  ending with `#` (`{R% ... %}#`) also runs during lookahead, except in an
  expansion that is a single token, which is scanned without its code, as in
  the other targets.
* **In a lexical action**, `self` is the `Lexer`, and the code runs in a
  `match` arm where `matched_token` (an `Option<Token>`; set it to `None` to
  drop the token), `matched_type` (a `TokenType`) and `token_begin_offset`,
  `match_start` and `match_end` (`i32` code point offsets) are local
  variables.  The fields injected with `INJECT LEXER_CLASS` are
  `self.<name>` in snake_case; `self.char_at(i)` returns the `char` at a code
  point offset.

